# Line endings for the files that run on the device.
#
# A CRLF in a device shell script is silent and total. busybox reads the
# carriage return as part of the command name, so every line fails with
# "not found" and the script exits 127. At boot that means a service never
# starts, and the only symptom is a port that does not answer. Nothing is
# logged that names the cause.
#
# core.autocrlf=true on a Windows checkout produces exactly that, so the rules
# below pin the working-tree ending whatever the local setting is.
#
# The default is now the whole repository, and the exceptions are named at the
# end. Enumerating the paths that needed the rule was tried first and it failed
# four times in one day, which is recorded in the tools/ comment further down.
# The same argument applies to the repository: a file is exempt because somebody
# decided it is exempt, not because nobody thought of its extension.
#
# `git ls-files --eol` measured what this costs. Six blobs in the index hold
# CRLF and every one is an upstream C or C++ source under `support/sg2002/`.
# Those six are marked `-text` at the end of this file, so no conversion touches
# them and no commit renormalises files this repository does not own. Every
# other tracked blob is already LF, so the default below changes no blob at all.
# It changes what a fresh checkout writes, which is the failure being prevented.
#
# Adding this rule does not repair a working tree that is already CRLF. Git
# applies attributes when it writes a file, so a file checked out before the
# rule existed keeps its carriage returns and reports clean.
#
# `git ls-files --eol` puts the three status fields in one tab-separated column
# and the path in the next, so the path is $2 under `-F'\t'` and the statuses
# have to be split out of $1 by hand. Splitting on whitespace instead makes
# `$1` and `$2` the first two statuses, the condition then matches every text
# file in the repository, and `$4` prints the tail of the attribute string. It
# reports a long list of `eol=lf` and looks like it worked. Find and repair
# them with:
#
#   git ls-files --eol \
#     | awk -F'\t' '{ split($1, s, " "); if (s[1] == "i/lf" && s[2] == "w/crlf") print $2 }' \
#     > stale.txt
#   while IFS= read -r f; do rm -f "$f"; done < stale.txt
#   git checkout -- $(tr '\n' ' ' < stale.txt)
#
# Run on 2026-08-19 this found 133 files, among them
# `kvmapp/system/init.d/S03usbhid`, which is a device init script and therefore
# the exact silent failure described at the top of this file. No blob changed,
# which is the point: the index was always LF and only the checkout was wrong.

* text=auto eol=lf

kvmapp/system/init.d/*      text eol=lf
kvmapp/system/*.py          text eol=lf
kvmapp/system/*.sh          text eol=lf
kvmapp/jpg_stream/*         text eol=lf
kvmapp/picoclaw/**/*.sh     text eol=lf
kvmapp/picoclaw/**/*.md     text eol=lf
kvmapp/picoclaw/**/*.yaml   text eol=lf
# Everything under tools/ is a script a shell has to run, and enumerating them
# failed four times in one day: first tools/oled/S97oled-nudge (no extension, not
# in the directory that was matched), then support/sg2002/build, then
# docker/entrypoint, then tools/deploy/deploy-server. Stop guessing and cover the
# tree. text=auto still leaves real binaries alone, which matters because
# tools/nanokvm_update_edid/ has a compiled one committed in it.
tools/**                    text=auto eol=lf
tools/slots/*.inc           text eol=lf
tools/slots/*.its.in        text eol=lf
*.sh                        text eol=lf

# The builder image entry point. Docker execs it directly, and with CRLF the
# kernel looks for "/bin/sh\r" and the container dies with "exec /entrypoint: no
# such file or directory".
docker/entrypoint           text eol=lf

# Embedded into the server binary with //go:embed and written to
# /usr/share/udhcpc/default.script.d/ on the device, where busybox execs it on
# every DHCP event. A CRLF copy is a hook that can never run, and nothing about
# the failure would point at line endings.
server/service/network/scripts/*  text eol=lf

*.py                        text eol=lf

# The MaixCDK build entry point. It never reaches the device, but it is a script
# a shell has to execute: with CRLF the kernel execs "/bin/bash\r" and reports
# 127, which is how the builder image stopped building. The C and C++ sources
# beside it are deliberately left alone - upstream committed those with CRLF and
# renormalising them would rewrite files this repository does not own.
support/*/build             text eol=lf

# Source that never reaches the device, pinned for the tools that read the
# working tree instead of the index. gofmt and prettier both count a carriage
# return as a formatting difference, so a Windows checkout with
# core.autocrlf=true makes them flag almost everything: 224 of the 249 Go files
# and all 213 TypeScript files. A formatter that reports every file reports
# nothing, and a real formatting mistake hides in that noise.
#
# The index already holds these as LF, so the rule pins the checkout only. It
# rewrites no blob, and it moves nothing away from upstream.
*.go                        text eol=lf
*.ts                        text eol=lf
*.tsx                       text eol=lf

# Read by the riscv64 cross-compiler out of the working tree, through a
# mounted Docker volume, not out of the index. A checkout with
# core.autocrlf=true is exactly what feeds that mount, so without this rule a
# fresh clone can hand the compiler headers with CRLF in them.
server/include/opus/*.h     text eol=lf

# Device payloads that must stay byte-exact.
*.ko                        binary
*.so                        binary
*.so.*                      binary
kvmapp/kvm_system/*         binary
kvmapp/kvm_new_app          binary
# The only .a in the repository, sitting beside the .so files above it in
# server/dl_lib/. Same reason: a device payload, not source, and it must not
# be run through any text conversion.
*.a                         binary

# The six exceptions the default at the top of this file refers to. Upstream
# committed these with CRLF, and the blobs still hold it. `-text` turns every
# conversion off for them, in both directions: a checkout writes the blob
# unchanged, and a commit stores the working tree unchanged. Without it, the
# default `text=auto` would rewrite each one to LF the next time it is staged,
# which would put a whitespace-only diff on files this repository does not own
# and would make every later merge from upstream conflict on all of them.
#
# They are compiled, never executed by a shell and never copied to the device,
# so their line endings cost nothing. Listed one per line rather than matched by
# a directory glob, because the neighbouring sources in the same directories are
# LF and must stay that way.
support/sg2002/kvm_system/main/include/config.h             -text
support/sg2002/kvm_system/main/lib/hdmi/hdmi.cpp            -text
support/sg2002/kvm_system/main/lib/hdmi/hdmi.h              -text
support/sg2002/kvm_system/main/lib/oled_ctrl/oled_ctrl.h    -text
support/sg2002/kvm_system/main/src/main.cpp                 -text
support/sg2002/kvm_vision_test/main/src/main.cpp            -text
